<LearningGuideLayout title="How to Add Animation Frame Events in Godot" description="Trigger sounds, hitboxes, particles, camera changes, and gameplay events at exact animation times in Godot 4 and FPE." summary="Animation frame events synchronize discrete gameplay actions with a timeline: a footstep lands, a tool strikes, a door clears its frame, or a camera cuts. They are for moments—not continuous motion." relatedSlugs={["godot-trigger-system","dialogue-triggers-godot","blender-to-godot-gameplay-import"]} godotSource={{label:"Animation track types",href:"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/animation/animation_track_types.html"}}>
    <div class="guide-summary"><strong>Godot's built-in tool is a Call Method Track.</strong> Add keys at precise times and call a small relay method. The relay emits a semantic event so animation data does not need to know the whole gameplay system.</div>
    <h2>When should you use frame events?</h2><p>Use them when timing belongs to the authored animation: footsteps, impact windows, particles, audio cues, prop release, or cutscene beats. Use an animation-finished signal for end-of-clip state changes. Do not use frame events to update a value continuously; property tracks already do that better.</p>
    <h2>The traditional Godot workflow</h2><ol><li>Select the animation in <code>AnimationPlayer</code>.</li><li>Add a <strong>Call Method Track</strong> targeting a stable relay node.</li><li>Insert a key at the exact moment and call a method such as <code>animation_event(&amp;"FOOTSTEP")</code>.</li><li>Have that relay emit a signal or dispatch the named event to the owning character or scene.</li><li>Test forward, looping, blended, and interrupted playback.</li></ol>
    <pre><code>signal frame_event(event_id: StringName)

func animation_event(event_id: StringName) -&gt; void:
    frame_event.emit(event_id)</code></pre>
    <h2>Why use a relay?</h2><p>A method track can call any method, but directly calling <code>delete_enemy()</code> or <code>load_next_level()</code> makes the animation difficult to reuse. Calling one stable relay with a named event keeps the clip portable. It also gives you one place to log, ignore stale events, or translate authored names into gameplay commands.</p>
    <h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li><strong>Expecting preview calls:</strong> Godot does not execute method-track calls while previewing in the editor.</li><li><strong>Destructive immediate callbacks:</strong> deferred method-call mode is safer when events modify the scene tree or animation player.</li><li><strong>Polling the current frame:</strong> fast playback and frame-rate changes can skip equality checks.</li><li><strong>Forgetting interruption:</strong> decide whether cancelling an attack also clears its pending hit window.</li></ul>
    <Fragment slot="fpe"><p>In Blender's Action Editor, move to the desired frame and add an FPE <strong>Frame Event</strong> with a stable Scene Event name such as <code>PLAY_CHIME</code>. FPE carries that timing through glTF, attaches a <code>FrameEventRunner</code> when the level loads, and dispatches the matching event during playback. That event can run the same command catalog used by triggers.</p><p><a href="/docs/fpe-animation-and-events.html">Read the animation and frame events reference</a>.</p></Fragment>
</LearningGuideLayout>
